<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.keel.live</string>

    <!-- NOTE ON COMMENT STYLE. The rest of this repo separates asides with a double hyphen. XML
         forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, and while Apple's CoreFoundation parser is
         lenient enough to accept one (plutil -lint says OK), a strict parser such as expat does
         not, which makes the file unreadable to ordinary XML tooling and to the tests that assert
         on the schedule below. So this one file uses other punctuation. Do not "fix" it back.

         DAILY SUPERVISED-LIVE cycle on the real money path. Runs one HEADLESS cycle to check
         whether a Turtle breakout fired. Whether it PLACES the resulting order or merely notifies
         depends on the AUTONOMY flag on the profile row, read fresh from the database each cycle
         (see keel-live-run.sh's header); with autonomy ON it places, unattended.

         ANCHORED TO UTC, NOT TO LOCAL TIME. Daily candles close at 00:00 UTC, but
         turtle_breakout._completed_days withholds the just-closed daily bar until the 00:00 to
         01:00 UTC HOURLY bar has closed, so 01:00 UTC is the earliest instant at which a cycle
         sees fresh data. keel-live-run.sh gates on UTC hour >= 1 and stamps the UTC date. This
         used to sit at 09:05 LOCAL, i.e. 13:05/14:05 UTC, which cost roughly twelve hours of
         avoidable lag on every breakout for no benefit whatsoever.

         WHY :20 AND NOT :00. Twenty minutes of margin for Coinbase to publish the 00:00 to 01:00
         UTC hourly candle and for data.market_feed to persist it. That candle is the thing that
         releases the fresh daily bar; a trigger at exactly 01:00 UTC would race the very bar it
         exists to consume. The trigger nearest the boundary is 01:20 UTC.

         WHY 24 TRIGGERS. The count is CATCH-UP BREADTH, not cadence. launchd re-runs a missed
         StartCalendarInterval on wake from SLEEP but NOT when the trigger passed while the
         machine was powered OFF, so a shutdown over the scheduled hour silently lost the detector
         for the day (observed 2026-07-28: booted 09:24, `launchctl print` reported runs = 0). The
         only defence is another trigger later, so there is one every hour; the catch-up window is
         now 23 hours rather than 12. Since these are local hours and the gate is on UTC, every
         UTC date gets a trigger in every UTC hour, so an eligible one always exists, including
         across both DST transitions.

         ONCE PER UTC DAY is enforced by keel-live-run.sh's day-stamp, NOT by the trigger count.
         For any UTC date X the newest visible daily bar is constant (it is X-1) across the whole
         eligible window, so whichever eligible trigger fires first evaluates bar X-1 and stamps
         X; later triggers that UTC day are no-ops, and a FAILED cycle leaves no stamp so the next
         hour retries it. That stamp is a CORRECTNESS mechanism: nothing on the live path dedupes
         an entry, so two cycles in one UTC day means two entries off one daily bar. See
         tests/test_schedule.py, which pins all of this.

         This changes WHEN a cycle runs, never whether it may place an order, which is autonomy's
         job alone. This comment used to say "the detector still places nothing: confirm mode
         fails closed with no TTY", which held only with autonomy OFF while the deployment has run
         with it ON. Do not restore that wording without checking the profile row first. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/elmehdiaitbrahim/keel/keel-live-run.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
